1. Opening and why AI matters (0:00–4:00)
[Walk to the front. Smile. Pause two seconds before you speak.]
Good morning everyone, and thank you all for being here. I'm Praveen, from the VBAM team.
Today is the first session in a new series on Copilot. Think of it as a refresher — the basics, done properly, so we all start from the same place. And we have a lot more to come, so this is just the beginning.
Quick show of hands. Who here spent more than half an hour this week on something like summarising a long email chain, tidying a spreadsheet, or writing an update you've written a hundred times before? [Raise your own hand. Wait.]
Keep your hand up if you enjoyed it. [Pause. Let the hands drop and the laugh happen.]
That's what today is about. Not robots. Not coding. Just getting that half hour back.
Why AI, and why now
Here's what makes AI a strange technology. Every tool we've had at work made us learn its language. Excel, we learned formulas. Outlook, we learned rules and folders.
AI is the first tool that learns our language. You just type what you want, in plain English, the way you'd ask a colleague. If you can write an email, you can use AI. That's the only entry requirement.
And the payoff adds up fast. Say it saves you just 20 to 30 minutes a day — a first draft here, a summary there. That's about two hours a week. Over a year, that's two to three working weeks back.
[Pause. Let that number land.]
I'm not asking you to change how you work. I'm asking you to hand off the boring first 80 percent, so you spend your time on the part that actually needs you.
One picture to keep in mind for the rest of today. AI is like a very smart new joiner. Fast, eager, knows a lot about the world. But on day one it doesn't know our clients, our way of doing things, or what you want — unless you tell it. We'll come back to that.
So let me show you the tool we already have: Microsoft 365 Copilot.
[Switch to the Copilot app.]
2. Why use the Copilot app (4:00–5:30)
First, where to use it. You've probably seen Copilot pop up in Teams, in Outlook, and inside Word and Excel. Those are handy for quick things.
But my one request today: download the Microsoft 365 Copilot app and make it your home base.
Think of it this way. The Copilot in Teams and Outlook is the colleague you bump into in the corridor — good for a quick question. The app is that colleague's desk, with all their tools laid out. It has the newest updates and a lot more features. Everything I'll show you today — uploading files, saving prompts, scheduling them — lives in the app.
[Show where to get it at the firm, e.g. Company Portal or Microsoft Store. Confirm this with IT before the talk.]
Install it, pin it to your taskbar, and open it once a day. That habit alone is half the battle.
3. Is my data safe? (5:30–7:00)
Now the question everyone in finance should be asking: is it safe to put our data in here?
Short answer: yes, when you're signed in with your work account.
Here's how I think about it. A free AI tool on the internet is like talking in a coffee shop. You don't know who's listening, and what you say might be used to train the tool.
Copilot at our firm is a meeting room inside our own building. Microsoft gives every company its own locked space in its cloud. The tech word is "tenant" — just think "our firm's private room."
What you type and upload stays in that room. It's protected the same way our emails and files already are. And it is not used to train the AI.
Two more things worth knowing:
• Copilot only sees what you can already see. If you don't have access to a folder, neither does Copilot.
• Our normal rules still apply. Copilot doesn't change what we're allowed to do with client data. If you're unsure, check with [compliance contact] as you would today.
[If your screen shows a shield or "Protected" label near your profile, point to it: "That's your sign you're in the safe, work version."]
4. Home screen tour (7:00–10:30)
[Presenter note: since June 2026 the old Work/Web switch is a single Work IQ button, top left of the chat screen. On = mode 1, off = mode 2.]
This is the home screen. A box to type in, and not much else. That's on purpose — you just ask.
Two modes: Work IQ on or off
[Point to the Work IQ button, top left.]
See this button? It's called Work IQ, and it switches Copilot between two modes.
Work IQ on — Copilot can look at your work world. Your emails, Teams chats, meetings and files, plus the internet. So you can ask, "What did we agree in Tuesday's budget meeting?" and it can actually answer.
Work IQ off — Copilot only uses the public internet. Good for "Explain deferred revenue in simple terms" or "What are the latest changes to lease accounting?"
My rule of thumb: leave it on. Turn it off only when you want an answer purely from the internet, with nothing from our work mixed in.
OneDrive and SharePoint, in one breath
You'll hear two words a lot: OneDrive and SharePoint.
OneDrive is your personal drawer — your own files, saved online instead of on your laptop.
SharePoint is the team filing cabinet — the shared folders your team or department uses.
Copilot can read files from both, as long as you have access. So you can say "use the Q3 forecast file" and it can go find it. Or you can attach a file directly.
[Show the attach / + button in the chat box.]
The models, briefly
[Open the model picker.]
Last thing on this screen. This menu picks the "brain" behind Copilot. Microsoft gives us a few — some from OpenAI, the makers of ChatGPT, and some from Anthropic, the makers of Claude.
Honest advice: leave it on Auto. Auto picks the right brain for the job. If you have a harder problem — say, comparing two long reports — try Think deeper. It takes a little longer, but it reasons more carefully.
