5. Demo 1: from spreadsheet to ready-to-send email (10:30–16:30)
Enough talking. Let's do something real.
I've got a spreadsheet here — [describe it: e.g. "12 months of department spending"]. Normally I'd spend 30 to 40 minutes with filters and pivot tables just to understand it. Watch this.
[Attach the Excel file. Paste prompt 1.]
Analyse this file. Explain what the data shows in plain English and give me the 5 most important takeaways.
[While it works:] Notice I didn't use any special words. I typed exactly what I'd say to a colleague.
[Read two takeaways aloud. Paste prompt 2.]
Which 3 [departments / funds / regions] changed the most from last quarter? Show it as a table and a chart.
See — I didn't start over. I just kept the conversation going, like talking to a person.
[Paste prompt 3.]
Is there anything unusual, missing, or that looks like an error in this data? List the rows.
This is my favourite one. It's a second pair of eyes. But one rule: Copilot does the first draft, you do the final check. Always glance at the numbers before they go anywhere.
[Paste prompt 4.]
Draft a short email to my manager summarising these findings. Under 150 words, three bullet points, professional but friendly, and end with one recommended next step.
[Show the draft. Copy it into a new Outlook email.]
And that's ready to send. Read it once, add your name, hit send. About five minutes, instead of an hour.
Now picture your own version. HR, swap this for a headcount or attrition sheet. Marketing, campaign results. PMs, your project tracker. Same four steps: understand it, dig in, check it, write it up.
[If it's slow:] "The one thing it can't do yet is read my mind faster." [If it fails, switch to your screenshots and keep talking.]
6. Why prompts matter (16:30–18:00)
Everything you just saw came down to one thing: what I typed. That's called a prompt. A prompt is simply your instructions.
Remember our smart new joiner? If you tell a new joiner "do the report," you'll get a report. If you say "do the monthly variance report for the board, two pages, focus on the three biggest misses, by Friday" — you get the report you actually need.
Copilot is exactly the same. Better instructions, better answer.
Microsoft's own tip is to cover four things:
• Goal — what do you want?
• Context — why, and who is it for?
• Source — which files or information should it use?
• Expectations — what should the answer look like? Length, tone, format.
But here's the good news. You don't need to memorise any of that. Copilot can help write the prompt for you. Let me show you three levels.
7. Demo 2: three levels of prompting (18:00–24:30)
Level 1: just ask
[New chat. Paste:]
Write an email to my team about the year-end deadline.
[Show the result.]
It's… fine. It's polite. It's also the kind of email everyone skims and forgets. Copilot had to guess everything — which team, which deadline, what tone.
Level 2: let Copilot interview you
Now watch this small change. I give it the same request, but I ask it to help me first.
[New chat. Paste:]
I want to write an email to my team about the year-end deadline. Before you write anything, rewrite my request into a better prompt, and ask me any questions you need answered to do a great job.
[Show the questions it asks. Answer two or three out loud as you type them in, e.g. "Finance team of 12, deadline is 15 December, tone friendly but firm."]
See what happened? It interviewed me, the way a good colleague would before starting. And the email is now something I'd actually send.
Level 3: give it the file, let it write the prompt
This is the one I want you to remember.
[New chat. Attach a real document, e.g. the year-end process note or the Excel file from Demo 1. Paste:]
Read this file. I need to [brief my team on what they must do before year-end]. Write me the best possible prompt to get that done, using the details in this file. Ask me if anything is missing.
[Show the prompt it writes. Copy it, paste it into the box, and run it.]
That's the trick. You give Copilot the context, it writes the instructions, you press go. You get a prompt that fits your exact work, in seconds, without being a prompt expert.
Quick recap
1. Level 1 — just ask. Fine for quick things.
2. Level 2 — ask it to improve your prompt and ask you questions.
3. Level 3 — give it your file and ask it to write the prompt.
If you remember nothing else today, remember Level 2. Add "ask me any questions you need" to the end of your request. It changes everything.
8. Save it, then put it on autopilot (24:30–27:30)
[Presenter note: Microsoft now calls the old "Copilot Lab" the Copilot Prompt Gallery. Use that name so people can find it. Icons can differ slightly, so rehearse on your own screen.]
You've built a great prompt. Don't type it again next week. Two ways to reuse it.
Save it: your speed dial
[Hover over a prompt you just ran. Click the bookmark icon. Give it a short title, like "Weekly team summary". Click Save.]
That's it. It's now in your own prompt library.
[Start a new chat. Click the … More prompts button under the message box. Open "Your saved prompts". Click the one you saved.]
One click, and the whole prompt is back, ready to go. Your saved prompts are private to you. And if one works really well, you can share it with your team from the Prompt Gallery — so nobody has to reinvent it.
Schedule it: a standing order
Saving is speed dial. Scheduling is like a standing order at the bank — it just happens.
[Paste and run:]
Review my emails and Teams messages from the last 24 hours. Summarise the key updates in 3–5 bullet points, list any action items for me with due dates, and flag anything urgent that needs a reply today.
[Hover over the prompt. Click the clock icon, "Schedule this". Set the start time to 8:15 AM, tick Monday to Friday, set an end date, and tick the email alert. Click Create.]
Now every weekday at 8:15, before I've even opened Outlook, Copilot has read my inbox and told me what matters. That's my 20 minutes, back, every morning — without lifting a finger.
9. Close and call to action (27:30–30:00)
So, three things to take away:
1. Download the Copilot app and make it your home base.
2. It's safe. Your work stays in our firm's private room.
3. You don't need perfect prompts. Ask Copilot to ask you questions, or give it your file and let it write the prompt.
